   "client_rules": "outlook-2026-10",
   "reviewed": {
    "release": "0.13.1",
    "by": "Ian",
    "note": "Chosen to survive Outlook's own dark mode, which ignores our dark CSS when HTML is pasted and in classic Outlook: pure #FFFFFF surfaces become Outlook's dark canvas; every other background goes to a mid grey of the same hue; dark text flips light and turns toward magenta. So surfaces are pure white, the text accent is a deeper violet that flips to lilac, grape is used only for fills and rules (Outlook keeps saturated fills), and no text is lighter than muted. The dark palette (for clients that run our dark CSS) is set close to Outlook's result so the email looks alike everywhere. Check with the 'Outlook dark (simulated)' view in preview.png."
   },
   "departures": []
  },
  "_note": "Email and Office palette (D8). Core owns the client rules and checks (palette.js, outlook-sim.js); the brand approves these values. source_roles are the brand values it was derived from; run.sh palette and the swatch sheet report when one of them, or the client rules, has changed. Builds stay silent."
